1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:05,070
Think about the last time you deployed a feature in the production.

2
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:09,609
You probably tested it locally and crossed your fingers during the deployment.

3
00:00:09,850 --> 00:00:13,210
Now imagine doing that same in the project which is

4
00:00:13,210 --> 00:00:15,609
having a 40 plus microservices.

5
00:00:15,770 --> 00:00:20,129
Picture your GCP organization as a city, and in this city,

6
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:25,520
each environment like prod, stage is like a district with different risks,

7
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:29,840
with different rules, and with different levels of access.

8
00:00:30,069 --> 00:00:36,209
The production is the most secure district where everything here must be stable,

9
00:00:36,349 --> 00:00:38,229
monitored, and restricted.

10
00:00:38,470 --> 00:00:41,330
You have the strict IAM policy.

11
00:00:41,442 --> 00:00:44,462
It also has a restrictive organization policy,

12
00:00:44,962 --> 00:00:49,192
network isolation through shared VPC, stable version of services,

13
00:00:49,402 --> 00:00:54,301
longer retention policies, and high observability requirements.

14
00:00:54,562 --> 00:00:57,481
Google recommends broad isolation because the production

15
00:00:57,481 --> 00:01:00,881
environment must be protected from the drift and

16
00:01:00,881 --> 00:01:03,201
misconfiguration or privilege misuse.

17
00:01:03,692 --> 00:01:07,533
Staging is the production replica. Staging is where you

18
00:01:07,533 --> 00:01:09,484
simulate the real‑world production.

19
00:01:09,613 --> 00:01:11,594
It typically mirrors the production.

20
00:01:11,814 --> 00:01:14,814
It has the same VPC, same IAM structure,

21
00:01:15,054 --> 00:01:18,573
same firewall rules, same region and cluster sizes,

22
00:01:18,643 --> 00:01:21,134
and same version of dependencies.

23
00:01:21,333 --> 00:01:24,674
It's almost like exactly to your production environment

24
00:01:25,323 --> 00:01:28,880
without actually having the production risk. In dev

25
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:31,045
environments, engineers experiment, build,

26
00:01:31,625 --> 00:01:34,146
break the things, and try out the new features.

27
00:01:34,346 --> 00:01:37,106
These environments are intentionally flexible.

28
00:01:37,426 --> 00:01:41,286
Security is still important, but less restrictive,

29
00:01:41,466 --> 00:01:44,545
and mostly the developers get the higher privileges here.

30
00:01:44,825 --> 00:01:48,225
Most enterprises also have a shared service folder which is

31
00:01:48,225 --> 00:01:52,459
having the centralized CI/CD, Artifact Registry, central

32
00:01:52,459 --> 00:01:55,419
monitoring and logging, and service mesh.

33
00:01:55,589 --> 00:02:01,260
This keeps critical shared components isolated from individual applications.

34
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:04,139
Let's work through how code moves across the

35
00:02:04,139 --> 00:02:06,540
environment, a critical DevOps workflow.

36
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:10,258
Imagine you're working on the add new payment method

37
00:02:10,258 --> 00:02:11,800
feature for the payment service.

38
00:02:12,059 --> 00:02:13,759
Here is the typical lifecycle.

39
00:02:14,029 --> 00:02:17,210
First, the developer commits the code that triggered the CI.

40
00:02:17,330 --> 00:02:19,833
It will do the unit test, security scans,

41
00:02:20,003 --> 00:02:24,444
build Artifact Registry, and store images in the Artifact Registry.

42
00:02:24,794 --> 00:02:28,544
Then Cloud Deploy promotes the image into the development.

43
00:02:28,833 --> 00:02:32,194
This environment is meant for fast iteration.

44
00:02:32,464 --> 00:02:35,813
Developers can see the features live within minutes.

45
00:02:36,194 --> 00:02:37,753
Once the dev test passes,

46
00:02:38,014 --> 00:02:41,604
the pipeline promotes the release to the staging environment.

47
00:02:41,794 --> 00:02:45,363
Here your integration test will run, your E2E,

48
00:02:45,434 --> 00:02:47,697
that is end to end workflow gets tested.

49
00:02:47,968 --> 00:02:53,177
This gives the engineer confidence that the feature behaves as expected.

50
00:02:53,328 --> 00:02:56,037
In the next step, depending on the company,

51
00:02:56,307 --> 00:02:59,388
this may involve engineering or manager review,

52
00:02:59,567 --> 00:03:03,527
a product owner approval, SRE rollout readiness check.

53
00:03:03,727 --> 00:03:06,760
Once all this gets done, we move to step five,

54
00:03:07,328 --> 00:03:11,207
where finally the Cloud Deploy pushes the clean

55
00:03:11,207 --> 00:03:13,488
tested version to the production.

56
00:03:13,852 --> 00:03:14,682
At this point,

57
00:03:14,871 --> 00:03:18,662
Google Cloud recommends the release should be in the form of canary

58
00:03:18,662 --> 00:03:22,572
release or maybe blue‑green deployment or it may be the progressive

59
00:03:22,572 --> 00:03:24,302
rollout through the Cloud Deploy.

60
00:03:24,582 --> 00:03:29,561
Now let's talk about the real game changer, that is ephemeral environments.

61
00:03:29,811 --> 00:03:33,341
These are the temporary preview environments created per

62
00:03:33,341 --> 00:03:35,882
pull request or per feature branch.

63
00:03:36,182 --> 00:03:39,141
This will help the product manager to review the UI

64
00:03:39,141 --> 00:03:42,705
changes, QA to validate behavior before merge.

65
00:03:42,955 --> 00:03:48,055
You can deploy the previews without breaking the dev or the staging environment.

66
00:03:48,315 --> 00:03:52,195
Google's best practices describe it as creating on‑demand

67
00:03:52,195 --> 00:03:55,936
isolated environments for testing the features independent

68
00:03:55,936 --> 00:03:57,695
of the stable environment.

69
00:03:57,966 --> 00:04:01,106
So that's how you manage the multi‑environment and

70
00:04:01,106 --> 00:04:04,156
ephemeral deployments in the Google Cloud,

71
00:04:04,195 --> 00:04:07,996
the same way high‑performing DevOps teams do worldwide.
